#!markdown

## 0 · Switches and helpers — run this first, every time

Edit the values, then run. The cell then loads `notebooks/mr-load-helpers.ps1` (shared with
the run-sheet notebook `mr-load-run.dib`): `Invoke-Ubuntu '<bash command>'` runs one command
line in the repo, inside the Ubuntu of the chosen route, with the venv active. `-Live` prepends
`YES_ALL=1` (skips the runner's confirmation prompts) and only works when `$ConfirmLive` is true.
`-Stdin '<text>'` pipes text into the command (used for secrets). The helpers also set
`WSL_UTF8=1`: without it `wsl.exe` writes UTF‑16 when its output is captured, and every line in
this notebook would come out as `g.a.r.b.l.e.d`.

Once this machine is set up, switch to **`mr-load-run.dib`** for day-to-day execution: the 16
steps in order, a *where am I* cell, and `Invoke-NextStep`.

#!pwsh

# ── switches ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
$Route       = 'wsl'                                   # 'wsl' | 'devcontainer' | 'linux'
$Distro      = 'Ubuntu-24.04'                          # route wsl:  `wsl -l -v` shows the name
$RepoWsl     = '~/mir-load'                            # route wsl:  clone on the LINUX side (never /mnt/c/…)
$RepoWin     = "$HOME\mir-load"                        # route devcontainer: Windows-side clone (bind-mounted at /work)
$Image       = 'mrload-ubuntu'                         # route devcontainer: built from scripts/dev/Dockerfile
$RepoLinux   = '/workspaces/mir-load'                  # route linux: repo path when this notebook runs inside Ubuntu
$Branch      = 'claude/mr-load-library-system-dphpbj'
$Repo        = 'https://github.com/Wkayaobama/mir-load.git'
$ConfirmLive = $false                                  # flip to $true ONLY when you intend BigQuery/HubSpot writes

# ── load the shared helpers (notebooks/mr-load-helpers.ps1: Invoke-Ubuntu, Open-UbuntuTerminal, Set-EnvKey, Invoke-Step, Get-PipelineState …) ──
$candidates = @((Join-Path $RepoWin 'notebooks\mr-load-helpers.ps1'),
                (Join-Path $PWD 'mr-load-helpers.ps1'),
                (Join-Path $PWD 'notebooks\mr-load-helpers.ps1'),
                (Join-Path $RepoLinux 'notebooks/mr-load-helpers.ps1'))
if ($Route -eq 'wsl' -and (Get-Command wsl.exe -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)) {
    $linuxHome  = (wsl.exe -d $Distro -e bash -lc 'echo $HOME' | Select-Object -First 1).Trim()
    $candidates += "\\wsl$\$Distro" + ((($RepoWsl -replace '^~', $linuxHome) -replace '/', '\')) + '\notebooks\mr-load-helpers.ps1'
}
$helpers = $candidates | Where-Object { $_ -and (Test-Path $_) } | Select-Object -First 1
if (-not $helpers) { throw "mr-load-helpers.ps1 not found. Looked in: $($candidates -join ' | '). Set `$RepoWin to your Windows clone (or open the repo folder in VS Code) and re-run." }
Invoke-Expression (Get-Content -Raw $helpers)
"helpers: $helpers"
Show-Route

#!markdown

## 1 · Host prerequisites (Windows side)

Nothing here is fatal on its own; it tells you which routes are open on this machine.
`wsl` present → route A possible. `docker` present (Docker Desktop running) → route B possible.
`dotnet` is what the notebook extension itself needs.

#!pwsh

foreach ($t in 'wsl', 'docker', 'git', 'code', 'wezterm', 'wt', 'dotnet') {
    $c = Get-Command $t -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    '{0,-8} {1}' -f $t, $(if ($c) { $c.Source } else { '— not on PATH' })
}
''
'── WSL distros (VERSION must be 2) ──';        wsl.exe -l -v 2>$null
'── Docker engine ──';                          docker version --format 'server {{.Server.Version}} ({{.Server.Os}})' 2>$null
'── .NET SDKs (for Polyglot Notebooks) ──';     dotnet --list-sdks 2>$null
'── git line-ending setting on Windows ──';     'core.autocrlf = ' + (git config --global core.autocrlf)
'   (any value is fine: the repo''s .gitattributes pins LF for scripts; section 2/3 verifies the checkout)'

#!markdown

---
# Route A · WSL2 (local IDE, no Docker)

**Why WSL:** the clone lives on the Linux filesystem (`~/mir-load`), so the venv, the
SQLite ledger and the Drive download cache are fast and Linux-owned. VS Code opens the folder
through the *WSL* extension (`code --remote wsl+Ubuntu-24.04 …`) and runs Python, pytest and
the tasks inside the distro. Nothing from this route is visible to Windows Git, so a token in
`~/mir-load/.env` cannot be picked up by a Windows-side checkout by accident.

Skip to **Route B** if you prefer Docker. Both routes converge at **section 4 · .env clinic**.

### A1 · Install the distro (once)

`wsl --install` needs an elevated prompt (UAC will ask). It reboots the machine once, then
on first start Ubuntu asks for a **Linux username and password** — that password is the
`sudo` password the bootstrap asks for in A4. If `wsl -l -v` already shows the distro as
VERSION 2, skip this cell.

#!pwsh

wsl.exe --install -d $Distro          # once; reboot when asked, then start the distro once to create your Linux user
wsl.exe --set-default $Distro
wsl.exe -l -v

#!markdown

### A2 · Clone on the Linux side and check out the working branch

This runs `git` **inside** the distro, so the clone lands at `~/mir-load` with LF endings
regardless of any Windows Git setting. If the repository is private, Linux `git` will ask
for credentials and this cell will hang — run the same line in a terminal instead
(`Open-UbuntuTerminal`), or point WSL git at Windows' credential manager once:
`git config --global credential.helper "/mnt/c/Program\ Files/Git/mingw64/bin/git-credential-manager.exe"`.

#!pwsh

wsl.exe -d $Distro -e bash -lc "if [ -d $RepoWsl/.git ]; then echo 'clone present'; else git clone $Repo $RepoWsl; fi && cd $RepoWsl && git fetch -q origin $Branch && git checkout -q $Branch && git pull -q --ff-only origin $Branch && echo 'HEAD:' && git log --oneline -1"

#!markdown

### A3 · Line-ending sanity

Expected: `✔ checkout is LF-clean`. A CRLF checkout is the single most common reason a bash
script "does not exist" or dies on `set -euo pipefail\r`. The script prints the one-time fix if needed.

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/dev/crlf_check.sh'

#!markdown

### A4 · Bootstrap — **TERMINAL**

`scripts/bootstrap_ubuntu.sh` installs apt packages, the Google Cloud CLI (`gcloud`, `bq`),
creates `.venv`, installs the Python requirements (dbt-bigquery included), runs the unit
tests and creates `.env` from the template. It calls `sudo apt-get` itself, and `sudo` asks
for your Linux password on a terminal — that is why it cannot run from this cell.

Two rules that came from real mistakes:
- Run it **inside Ubuntu**. "Sudo is disabled on this machine" means the command ran in
  Git Bash/PowerShell on Windows (that sentence is Windows 11's own `sudo.exe`).
- Run it **without** `sudo bash …` — the script elevates only `apt`; the venv and `.mrload/`
  must stay owned by you, and the script refuses to run as root.

The cell opens the terminal; paste the two lines it prints. `--no-rehearsal` skips the 30 s
mock rehearsal (run it later from section 8 if you like).

#!pwsh

Open-UbuntuTerminal
@"

paste into the Ubuntu terminal that just opened:

    cd $RepoWsl
    bash scripts/bootstrap_ubuntu.sh --no-rehearsal

"@

#!markdown

### A5 · Verify the bootstrap

Expected: a Python 3.x from `.venv`, a gcloud version line, `bq` and `dbt` versions and
`… passed` from pytest. Anything missing → re-run A4 and read its first ✖ line.

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'which python && python --version && gcloud --version | head -1 && bq version 2>/dev/null | head -1 && dbt --version | sed -n 1,2p && python -m pytest tests -q --ignore=tests/e2e 2>&1 | tail -1'

#!markdown

### A6 · Open the WSL clone in VS Code (optional, for breakpoints and tasks)

Opens a VS Code window connected to the distro. Interpreter, tasks (`Ctrl+Shift+P` → *Tasks:
Run Task* → `mr-load: …`) and launch configurations come from the repo's `.vscode/`.
Do **not** open `\\wsl$\…\mir-load` as a plain Windows folder: Windows editors would write
CRLF into `.env` and scripts.

#!pwsh

$linuxHome = (wsl.exe -d $Distro -e bash -lc 'echo $HOME' | Select-Object -First 1).Trim()
$folder    = $RepoWsl -replace '^~', $linuxHome
code --remote "wsl+$Distro" $folder
"opened $folder in VS Code (WSL: $Distro)"

#!markdown

---
# Route B · Docker Desktop → Dev Container

**Why Docker:** one image (`scripts/dev/Dockerfile`, Ubuntu 24.04 + gcloud/bq + venv baked at
`/opt/venv`) that behaves identically on any laptop and matches the VM/Cloud Shell routes.
The repo stays on Windows and is bind-mounted at `/work`; gcloud credentials live in the
named volume `mrload-gcloud`, so you log in **once** and both the `docker run` cells below and
the VS Code Dev Container see the same credentials.

Two things to know on this route:
- The Windows checkout **must be LF** (B3 checks; the image strips `\r` from the bootstrap, and
  `scripts/ubuntu_shell.sh` refuses a CRLF checkout — same protection, host side).
- `.env` sits on NTFS. `chmod 600` has no effect through the mount, so the clinic reports the
  mode as "not enforceable here" — expected. The file is still git-ignored and hook-guarded.

Set `$Route = 'devcontainer'` in the Switches cell (and re-run it) before the cells below.

### B1 · Clone on the Windows side

#!pwsh

if ($Route -ne 'devcontainer') { Write-Warning "Switches cell says Route='$Route' — set it to 'devcontainer' and re-run it" }
if (-not (Test-Path "$RepoWin\.git")) { git clone $Repo $RepoWin }
git -C $RepoWin fetch -q origin $Branch
git -C $RepoWin checkout -q $Branch
git -C $RepoWin pull -q --ff-only origin $Branch
'HEAD: ' + (git -C $RepoWin log --oneline -1)

#!markdown

### B2 · Build the image (once; minutes the first time, seconds after)

Same Dockerfile the Dev Container uses. Rebuild after `requirements*.txt` changes.

#!pwsh

docker build -t $Image -f "$RepoWin\scripts\dev\Dockerfile" $RepoWin
docker image inspect $Image --format 'image {{.RepoTags}} built {{.Created}}'

#!markdown

### B3 · Line-ending sanity of the Windows checkout

Expected `✔ checkout is LF-clean`. If not, the next cell applies the one-time fix — it runs
`git reset --hard`, so it refuses while you have uncommitted changes.

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/dev/crlf_check.sh'

#!pwsh

# one-time LF fix for a clone made before .gitattributes existed (only if B3 reported CRLF)
if (git -C $RepoWin status --porcelain) { Write-Warning "uncommitted changes in $RepoWin — commit or stash first; not touching the checkout" }
else {
    git -C $RepoWin add --renormalize .
    git -C $RepoWin rm -r -q --cached .
    git -C $RepoWin reset -q --hard
    Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/dev/crlf_check.sh'
}

#!markdown

### B4 · Verify the image

Expected: python from `/opt/venv`, gcloud, bq, dbt, and pytest `… passed`.

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'which python && python --version && gcloud --version | head -1 && bq version 2>/dev/null | head -1 && dbt --version | sed -n 1,2p && python -m pytest tests -q --ignore=tests/e2e 2>&1 | tail -1'

#!markdown

### B5 · The VS Code Dev Container (optional, for breakpoints and tasks)

`code <clone>` then `F1` → **Dev Containers: Reopen in Container**. VS Code builds the same
Dockerfile, mounts the repo and the `mrload-gcloud` volume, and runs
`scripts/bootstrap_ubuntu.sh --no-rehearsal` as the post-create step (unit tests, `.env` from
the template). The status bar shows `Dev Container: mr-load`; the terminal is Ubuntu bash.
`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` also installs the .NET SDK feature, so this very notebook can
be opened inside the container — then use `$Route = 'linux'`.

#!pwsh

code $RepoWin
"VS Code opened on $RepoWin → F1 → 'Dev Containers: Reopen in Container'"

#!markdown

---
# 4 · `.env` clinic — configure secrets without ever committing them

**Precedence** (`scripts/run_pass1.sh`, `gauth.sh`, `auth_check.sh` all follow it):
process env **>** `.env` (in the repo) **>** `.env.mrload` **>** `../.env.mrload`. Files never
override a variable already present in the process environment, so `VAR=x scripts/run_pass1.sh …`
and CI always win.

**Required keys:** `MRLOAD_BQ_PROJECT` (BigQuery project; also the quota project for Drive calls)
and `HUBSPOT_SANDBOX_TOKEN` (`pat-eu1-…` / `pat-na1-…`, private app with files + companies +
notes + deals scopes). Set `HUBSPOT_SANDBOX_PORTAL_ID` too: `preflight` resolves the token to a
portal id and **dies** if it is `9201667` (WISeKey SA / ICALPS **production**, unless
`MRLOAD_ALLOW_PROD_PORTAL=1`) or if it differs from the id you configured — a copied token
cannot hit the wrong portal. The clinic and the auth check flag the same two conditions.

**What the clinic checks** (masked output only): which env files exist and win; missing keys;
template placeholders left in place; CRLF line endings (a Windows editor turns
`MRLOAD_BQ_PROJECT=x` into `x\r` → gcloud says "project not found"); file mode; and git safety
(`.gitignore` rule, nothing tracked or staged, pre-commit guard present). `--fix` creates
`.env` from the template, strips CRLF and sets mode 600.

### 4.1 · First pass with `--fix`

Expected on a fresh machine: `created .env from .env.mrload.example`, then ✖ for the two
placeholders. That is the "to-do list" the next cells clear.

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/dev/env_clinic.sh --fix'

#!markdown

### 4.2 · Enter the values (masked prompts)

Each cell shows a prompt at the top of VS Code. The token prompt is masked; the value is
piped into `scripts/dev/env_set.sh`, which replaces the key's line in `.env` (uncommenting the
template line if that is all there is), writes LF only, and prints just the key and the length.

If the prompt does not appear on your build of the extension, run the same thing in a terminal
(`Open-UbuntuTerminal`, then `scripts/dev/env_set.sh HUBSPOT_SANDBOX_TOKEN` — it asks silently).

#!pwsh

Set-EnvKey MRLOAD_BQ_PROJECT -Plain

#!pwsh

Set-EnvKey HUBSPOT_SANDBOX_TOKEN

#!pwsh

Set-EnvKey HUBSPOT_SANDBOX_PORTAL_ID -Plain

#!markdown

Other keys use the same helper, e.g. `Set-EnvKey MRLOAD_BQ_LOCATION -Plain` or, for the
service-account alternative to `gauth.sh`, `Set-EnvKey GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS -Plain`
with a path **inside Ubuntu** (`/home/<you>/keys/sa.json` on WSL, `/work/…` on Docker — and the
scope root folder must be shared with the service-account email, otherwise the walk is silently empty).

### 4.3 · Second pass — must be clean

Expected: every REQUIRED line ✔, `0 blocking issue(s)`. On the Docker route a single ⚠ about
the file mode is expected (NTFS through the mount).

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/dev/env_clinic.sh'

#!markdown

### 4.4 · Git safety: install the guard and prove it

The pre-commit hook refuses any staged `.env*` (except `.env.mrload.example`) and any staged
content that looks like a HubSpot token, a Google API key, an OAuth access token or a
service-account `"private_key"`. The second cell (`scripts/dev/guard_proof.sh`) prints the
`.gitignore` rule that matches `.env` (so a plain `git add .` can never pick it up), counts
tracked env files (0), then force-stages `.env`, tries to commit, expects the hook to refuse,
and unstages the file again. Expected last line: `✔ commit refused by the guard (rc=1)`.

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/dev/install_git_hooks.sh'

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/dev/guard_proof.sh'

#!markdown

---
# 5 · Google authentication (Drive + BigQuery, as you) — **TERMINAL**, then a check cell

`scripts/gauth.sh` does, in order: `gcloud config set project $MRLOAD_BQ_PROJECT` →
`gcloud auth login --enable-gdrive-access --update-adc` → `gcloud auth application-default
set-quota-project $MRLOAD_BQ_PROJECT` → enable the Drive and BigQuery APIs → a live GET of the
scope root folder and a `select 1` on BigQuery.

Why that exact sequence: `gcloud auth application-default login --scopes=…drive…` is
**rejected** by Google for gcloud's own OAuth client (you saw the permission error); the
`--enable-gdrive-access --update-adc` form is allowed and writes the Drive-scoped user
credentials to the same ADC file the pipeline reads. User credentials calling a non-Cloud API
(Drive) need a **quota project**, or the first call fails with "requires a quota project".

It prints a URL to open in your normal browser and asks you to paste the code back — a
terminal step. Where the credentials land: WSL → `~/.config/gcloud/` in the distro;
Docker → the `mrload-gcloud` volume (shared with the Dev Container).

#!pwsh

Open-UbuntuTerminal
$cd = if ($Route -eq 'wsl') { "cd $RepoWsl`n    " } else { '' }
@"

paste into the Ubuntu terminal:

    ${cd}scripts/gauth.sh

answer the consent screen fully (BigQuery AND Drive), paste the code back, wait for '✔ Drive OK' and '✔ BigQuery OK'.

"@

#!markdown

### 5.1 · Non-interactive credential check (re-run any time something "stopped working")

Expected: active account (masked), core/project = `MRLOAD_BQ_PROJECT`, ADC with a quota project,
`Drive: scope root readable → 20 opportunities and customer data`, `BigQuery: query OK`, and the
HubSpot portal id from the token (must **not** be 9201667).

| symptom | meaning | fix |
|---|---|---|
| `requires a quota project` | ADC has no quota project | re-run `gauth.sh` (step 3) |
| `insufficientPermissions` / `ACCESS_TOKEN_SCOPE_INSUFFICIENT` | consent screen accepted without Drive | re-run `gauth.sh`, tick both scopes |
| Drive `404` on the root | this Google account cannot see the folder | log in with the account that owns the Drive |
| `accessNotConfigured` | Drive API disabled on the project | `gcloud services enable drive.googleapis.com --project=<MRLOAD_BQ_PROJECT>` |
| HubSpot `401` | token wrong/expired or copied with CRLF | 4.2 again, then 4.3 |

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/dev/auth_check.sh'

#!markdown

---
# 6 · Pass 1 — the pipeline, step by step

Every step is `scripts/run_pass1.sh <step>`: idempotent, logged to `.mrload/logs/<step>-<ts>.log`,
and every **LIVE** step first re-runs its dry counterpart and asks for confirmation (`-Live`
answers yes through `YES_ALL=1`, and only when `$ConfirmLive` is true). Gates are set inline by
the script, never exported globally, so a stray shell can never fire a write.

### 6.0 · preflight
Verifies tools, `.env`, Drive access to the scope root, the HubSpot token and portal, BigQuery
access. Expected: only ✔ lines.

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/run_pass1.sh preflight'

#!markdown

### 6.1 · walk — Drive API depth-first walk → `.mrload/library_hierarchy.csv`
Pre-order DFS from the scope root (`30 Sales / 20 opportunities and customer data`), excluding
`70 Tradeshows`; every node gets `node_key` / `parent_key` (path as index), the company
node key, the legacy id (`3020Q…`) and an asset class (`deal_candidate` for PO/Billing PDFs,
`parked_for_review` for other PDFs, `shortcut`, `asset`). Also writes `silver_preview.csv`
(the 19-column icalps-parity preview, offline). Expected: a node count and no `ERROR`.

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/run_pass1.sh walk'
Invoke-Ubuntu 'wc -l .mrload/library_hierarchy.csv .mrload/silver_preview.csv && head -3 .mrload/library_hierarchy.csv | cut -c1-160'

#!markdown

### 6.2 · bq-init — datasets and empty ledger tables (so dbt sources resolve)

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/run_pass1.sh bq-init'

#!markdown

### 6.3 · bq-load — **LIVE** (gate `MRLOAD_APPROVE_BQ_LOAD`)
Loads the hierarchy CSV into `mrload_raw.library_hierarchy` with the positional schema
`pipeline/library_files/sql/library_hierarchy.schema.json`. Re-runnable (truncate + load).

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/run_pass1.sh bq-load' -Live

#!markdown

### 6.3b · hs-props — **LIVE** (gate `MRLOAD_APPROVE_PROPERTY_CREATE`), definitions only
Creates the HubSpot property **definitions** declared in `context/cards/library.yaml`
(`hubspot.properties`): group `mrload_library` on companies, notes and deals, prefix `mrload_`.
No values are written here; the values reach HubSpot through StackSync (BigQuery silver →
HubSpot, matched on the record ids that `ledger-export` writes back), and StackSync cannot map a
property that does not exist. Idempotent: `exists` everywhere on a re-run. An existing definition
with another type is reported as `type_mismatch` and never modified. A `403` names the missing
schema-write scope of the private app.

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/run_pass1.sh hs-props' -Live

#!markdown

### 6.4 · dbt — run + test: **the cardinality gate**
Builds `stg_library_hierarchy`, `silver_library_company`, `silver_library_index`,
`_deal_candidates`, `_parked`, `_orphans` on BigQuery and runs the contract tests (unique
node keys, every parent exists, one company per company node, …). **If any test fails, stop
here** — nothing downstream fires until the index is right. `scripts/dev/dbt_failures.sh`
(section 9) lists the failing nodes and their compiled SQL.

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/run_pass1.sh dbt'

#!markdown

### 6.4b · hs-props-verify — definitions vs the silver models as built (no gate)
Reads `dbt/target/catalog.json` (the `dbt` step runs `dbt docs generate` between run and test) and checks every
declared property twice: the HubSpot definition exists with the declared type, and the mapped
column exists in the built silver model. Writes `.mrload/review/stacksync_mapping.csv`: one
`match_key` row and one `property` row per object type, which is exactly what you enter in the
StackSync UI (source = `bigquery_table`, match = `bigquery_column` ↔ Record ID, fields =
`bigquery_column` → `hubspot_property`). Expected: `ok` on every row.

#!pwsh

Invoke-Ubuntu 'scripts/run_pass1.sh hs-props-verify'
Invoke-Ubuntu 'head -5 .mrload/review/stacksync_mapping.csv | cut -c1-180'

#!markdown

### 6.5 · review — operator queues (offline)
Writes `.mrload/review/`: `companies.csv` (the company objects that will be searched or
created — names are folder names), `deal_candidates.csv` (PO / Billing PDFs, pass 2),
`parked_for_review.csv` (other PDFs), `orphans.csv` (files with no company folder, never
attached), `multi_parent.csv` (files linked into two or more folders — must be empty before
going live) and the `deal_decisions.csv` template you edit before pass 2.

