# 10 · From sandbox to production — when you decide so

1. Keep the variable name: `HUBSPOT_SANDBOX_TOKEN` holds whichever portal's token you are
   targeting (`Set-EnvKey HUBSPOT_SANDBOX_TOKEN`, `Set-EnvKey HUBSPOT_SANDBOX_PORTAL_ID -Plain`).
2. Keep the ledgers apart, so the sandbox ledger can never mark a production file as "already
   uploaded": `Set-EnvKey MRLOAD_STATE_DIR -Plain` → `.mrload-prod`, and the same tree for
   `MRLOAD_LEDGER_PATH` (`.mrload-prod/ledger.sqlite`) and `MRLOAD_CACHE_DIR` (`.mrload-prod/cache`),
   because the template sets those two explicitly.
3. Re-run 5.1 (`auth_check.sh`) — it prints the portal id the token resolves to. If production is
   portal 9201667, `preflight` stops until `MRLOAD_ALLOW_PROD_PORTAL=1` is in `.env`
   (`Set-EnvKey MRLOAD_ALLOW_PROD_PORTAL -Plain`): the guard exists to stop the copied-token
   mistake, not to forbid a deliberate choice — but the choice is made in `.env`, on purpose.
4. Run section 6 again from `preflight`, dry steps first, `$ConfirmLive` last.
